#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fleetctl — run N brokers on N single-GPU rentals as ONE operation.

    .venv/bin/python fleetctl plan   -n 4                     # costs, no spend
    .venv/bin/python fleetctl up     -n 4 --scene <blend>
    .venv/bin/python fleetctl submit -n 4 --scene <blend> --frames 1-64 \
                                     --name proof --res 3840 2160 --samples 512
    .venv/bin/python fleetctl status
    .venv/bin/python fleetctl verify --manifest state/fleet/proof.json
    .venv/bin/python fleetctl down                            # and PROVE it

WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT BROKER CODE
----------------------------------------------
Measured 2026-08-07 (docs/multi-gpu.md), for the 2,978-frame master:

    architecture                    wall     days   total $    new code
    1 broker, 1 card (today)       163.1 h    6.8   $ 74.11        -
    8 brokers, 8 cards              20.4 h    0.8   $ 74.21      NONE
    1 broker, 8 workers, 8x box     30.4 h    1.3   $ 83.60    ~1,300 lines
    1 broker, 1 worker, 8x box     186.6 h    7.8  $512.05    none - DEFAULT

Eight broker processes on eight separate single-GPU rentals cost **ten cents
more** than one card and finish in a fifth of the time. They need no new broker
code because a second broker process is already live and proven: every
single-instance assumption inside `Fleet` — one `instance_id`, one tunnel, one
resident `scene_hash` — stays true *inside each process*.

What did not exist is the thing that launches, addresses, monitors and tears
down eight of them as ONE operation. That is this file. It writes no broker
code and imports no broker internals beyond reading the SQLite each broker
already keeps; every broker it starts is the shipping `broker.app` under the
shipping `scripts/brokerd.sh`.

THE FOUR THINGS IT REFUSES TO LET YOU GET WRONG
-----------------------------------------------
1. **Addressing.** `rq` takes a URL and nothing else, and it *silently
   re-routes* `anim`/`seq` to 8761 unless `VASTRENDER_URL` is set — so the
   obvious way to write a fleet submission sends all eight blocks to the
   client's live beat-1 card. Every address here comes off a `farm.brokers`
   object that carries its state directory and label with it, and every command
   prints the identity triple it used. R2-979 reported one broker's status as
   two; that is unreachable from here.

2. **The live two.** `fleet(n)` starts at broker 3, always. Brokers 1 and 2 are
   declared `protected` and no command in this file can start, stop, submit to
   or tear down either one.

3. **Double-render.** Blocks are contiguous and disjoint by construction, and
   `plan` refuses to emit a split whose blocks overlap or leave a gap.
   `verify` then proves it on the delivered PNGs by hash — which is the claim
   that matters, since a partition argument is about intent and a hash is about
   bytes.

4. **A partial teardown.** `down` is not finished when the brokers say so. It
   re-queries the vast.ai API and exits non-zero if ANY fleet instance is still
   alive, and names every non-fleet instance still billing so nobody reads
   "fleet down" as "farm down". An idle rented 5090 is $0.45/hr doing nothing,
   and that is the expensive failure mode of this whole design.
"""

